{#- The Main navigation bar. Included by base.njk on every page, and by
    content/unstrung/app/index.njk on the web app's page, which has none of the
    site's other styles: everything the bar needs is in assets/css/header.css
    and assets/js/menu.js.

    Every link has tabindex="0". Safari on a Mac leaves links out of the tab
    order unless its "Press Tab to highlight each item" setting is on, which
    left only the two dropdown buttons reachable by Tab, and no item inside a
    dropdown. An explicit tabindex puts a link in the order whatever that
    setting says. Other browsers already stop on links, so nothing changes
    there. base.njk and banner.njk do the same for the skip link and the banner.

    Every dropdown link has a title attribute repeating its text. header.css
    keeps each item to one line and cuts a long one off with an ellipsis; the
    title shows the whole text on hover, as on the side panels' links.

    The bar's own list is named "Main menu bar" (2026-11-21), so a screen
    reader names it when it is entered by Tab, arrows or single-letter
    navigation, not only "list". It stays a list: ARIA menu roles are for
    application menus, not site navigation. The <nav> has no label: it was
    named "Main", and NVDA read the two together as "main main menu bar". -#}
    <nav class="main-menu">
      <ul role="list" aria-label="Main menu bar">
        <li><a href="/" tabindex="0"{% if page.url == "/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Home</a></li>
        <li>
          <details class="submenu">
            <summary>Articles</summary>
            <ul role="list">
              <li><a href="/articles/" tabindex="0" title="All articles"{% if page.url == "/articles/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>All articles</a></li>
              {%- for article in collections.articles %}
              <li><a href="{{ article.url }}" tabindex="0" title="{{ article.data.title }}"{% if page.url == article.url %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ article.data.title }}</a></li>
              {%- endfor %}
            </ul>
          </details>
        </li>
        <li>
          <details class="submenu">
            <summary>unstrung</summary>
            <ul role="list">
              {#- From content/_data/unstrungPages.json. Until 2026-11-16 the
                  unstrung Pages side panel on /unstrung/ read it too, so a
                  page had one name in both places. #}
              {%- for item in unstrungPages %}
              <li><a href="{{ item.url }}" tabindex="0" title="{{ item.label }}"{% if page.url == item.url %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</a></li>
              {%- endfor %}
            </ul>
          </details>
        </li>
        {#- Read as "About eyesunstrung", so arrowing past the end of the
            unstrung dropdown does not sound like About unstrung. The visible
            text starts the name, as WCAG 2.5.3 asks. #}
        <li><a href="/about/" tabindex="0" aria-label="About eyesunstrung"{% if page.url == "/about/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>About</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
